## Layer 3: Terminal Renderer

### Approach: lightweight ANSI, not full TUI framework

A full TUI framework (ratatui) would be heavyweight for what is
essentially a progress display. Instead, use direct ANSI escape codes
with a layout engine that computes character positions from the DAG
topology.

Rationale:
- No external dependency (ratatui = ~15 crates in the dep tree)
- Output is a rectangular character buffer, easy to reason about
- Can print final state as static text after execution completes
- Works in any terminal that supports ANSI (which is all of them)

If we later want interactivity (scrolling, clicking nodes), we can
upgrade to ratatui — but the DagProgress model stays the same.

### Visual design

```
  gist-diff pipeline                               elapsed: 1.2s
  ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

  ● read_files ──────── ● parse_read_files ──────── ● render_markdown
    ✓ 0.02s               ✓ 0.1s  {234 entries}      ✓ 0.05s
                                        │
                                        ▼
                           ● prepare_gist_request ── ● execute_gist
                             ✓ 0.01s                   ◐ running...
                                                           │
                                                           ▼
                                                  ● parse_gist_response
                                                    ○ pending
```

### Node glyphs

    ○  Pending     (dim/gray)
    ◐  Running     (yellow, may animate ◐◑◒◓)
    ●  Completed   (green)
    ✗  Failed      (red)
    ◌  Skipped     (dim, strikethrough)
    ◇  Intercepted (cyan, dry-run)

### Edge glyphs

    ──  Idle     (dim gray)
    ══  Flowing  (bright white/yellow pulse)
    ──  Done     (green)
    ╌╌  Dead     (dim, dashed)

### Layout algorithm

Leveled layout based on topological distance from root:

    1. Compute topo order
    2. Assign each node a "level" = max distance from any root
    3. Within each level, order nodes by topo index
    4. Render left-to-right, wrapping at terminal width
    5. Draw edges as horizontal (same level) or vertical (cross-level)

For small DAGs (≤10 nodes), a single-line horizontal layout works:

    ● A ── ● B ── ● C ── ● D

For larger DAGs, use multi-row with vertical connections:

    ● A ──┬── ● B ── ● D ──┬── ● F
          │                 │
          └── ● C ──────────┘
                    │
                    └── ● E

### Rendering loop

```rust
pub struct TerminalRenderer {
    /// Layout computed from DagSnapshot
    layout: DagLayout,
    /// Current frame buffer (character grid)
    buffer: TermBuffer,
    /// Terminal dimensions
    width: u16,
    height: u16,
    /// Whether we're in "live" mode (with cursor movement)
    /// or "static" mode (just print lines)
    live: bool,
}

impl ProgressObserver for TerminalRenderer {
    fn on_dag_start(&mut self, snapshot: &DagSnapshot) {
        self.layout = compute_layout(snapshot, self.width);
        self.render_frame();
    }

    fn on_node_start(&mut self, node_id: &NodeId) {
        self.layout.set_state(node_id, NodeState::Running);
        self.render_frame();
    }

    fn on_node_complete(&mut self, node_id: &NodeId, summary: OutputSummary) {
        self.layout.set_state(node_id, NodeState::Completed);
        self.layout.set_summary(node_id, summary);
        // Transition outgoing edges to Flowing
        self.layout.flow_edges_from(node_id);
        self.render_frame();
    }

    fn on_dag_complete(&mut self, elapsed: Duration) {
        // Print final static summary (no cursor movement)
        self.print_final_summary(elapsed);
    }
}

impl TerminalRenderer {
    fn render_frame(&mut self) {
        if self.live {
            // Move cursor to top of display area and redraw
            print!("\x1b[{}A", self.buffer.height);
            self.buffer.clear();
            self.draw_dag();
            self.buffer.flush();
        } else {
            // Static mode: just print current state
            self.draw_dag();
            self.buffer.flush();
        }
    }
}
```

